{
  "name": "Schrödinger phase XOR",
  "description": "Four XOR gates where a bit is a soliton's phase, not whether it is there: 0 is phase 0, 1 is phase π (the colour shows phase). One gate per input pair (top left A=0 B=0, top right 0 1, bottom left 1 0, bottom right 1 1). In each, A (from lower left) and B (upper left) come in on paths 45° apart and cross. Different bits pass straight through each other, unchanged, into the two side boxes: the output is 1 when both side boxes hold a soliton. Equal bits attract: here they merge into one heavy soliton that goes on into the middle box. Measured in the lab, opposite phases pass through cleanly whatever the speed and with the nonlinearity changed ±2%, but the merge is fickle: change the nonlinearity by 1% (as a phone's GPU nearly can) and an equal pair may instead split unevenly, a big piece into the lower box and a small one towards the upper. So the reading is both-or-not: only a clean pass-through fills both. The traps (orange) are set to keep only what arrives near full strength (trap_loss 0.08, up from 0.02) and park it there; a fragment fades. Everything off the gates' paths absorbs (red). The inputs must arrive together, within about a cell, as in any billiard-ball logic. Answers by about step 9074 (19 s at 8 steps per frame). Try: change g a little in the rule and run it again, or pause at the start and erase an input to see a lone soliton go by.",
